{"id": "section-body-24073", "title": "Cancer of the Vulva", "chapterId": "chapter-twenty-two", "tocLevel": 4, "tocPath": ["APPROACH TO PATIENTS WITH EMERGENCY CONDITIONS", "MALIGNANT DISEASE CONDITIONS", "Gynecological Malignancies", "Cancer of the Vulva"], "blocks": [{"id": "body-24073", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Cancer of the Vulva", "runs": [{"text": "Cancer"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "the"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Vulva"}], "level": 4, "list": {"level": 2, "numberingId": "534"}}, {"id": "body-24074", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Vulva cancer is predominantly a disease of older women. Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common histological type, usually arising from premalignant lesions, such as vulva intraepithelial neoplasia (VIN). Risk factors contributing to the development of VIN and later vulva cancer include HPV infection, immunosuppression, including infection with HIV, history of genital warts, multiple sexual partners, and cigarette smoking.", "runs": [{"text": "Vulva cancer is predominantly a disease of older women. Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common histological type, usually arising from premalignant lesions, such as vulva intraepithelial neoplasia (VIN). Risk factors contributing to the development of VIN and later vulva cancer include HPV infection, immunosuppression, including infection with HIV, history of genital warts, multiple sexual partners, and cigarette smoking."}]}, {"id": "body-24077", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Clinical Presentation", "runs": [{"text": "Clinical"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Presentation"}], "level": 4}, {"id": "body-24078", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "Persistent itching in the vulva is often the earliest and most common symptom.", "runs": [{"text": "Persistent itching in the vulva is often the earliest and most common symptom."}], "list": {"level": 0, "numberingId": "488"}}, {"id": "body-24079", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "Vulvar mass or lesion—a lump, wart, or ulcerated or raised area.", "runs": [{"text": "Vulvar mass or lesion—a lump, wart, or ulcerated or raised area."}], "list": {"level": 0, "numberingId": "488"}}, {"id": "body-24080", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "Skin changes—thickening or discoloration (red, white, or dark spots)", "runs": [{"text": "Skin"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "changes—thickening"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "or"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "discoloration"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(red, white, or dark spots)"}], "list": {"level": 0, "numberingId": "488"}}, {"id": "body-24082", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Investigations", "runs": [{"text": "Investigations"}], "level": 4}, {"id": "body-24083", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "FBC, LFT, Urea, Creatinine, HIV test", "runs": [{"text": "FBC,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "LFT,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Urea,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Creatinine,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "HIV"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "test"}], "list": {"level": 0, "numberingId": "488"}}, {"id": "body-24084", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "Pap smear of cervix and vagina", "runs": [{"text": "Pap"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "smear"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "cervix"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "vagina"}], "list": {"level": 0, "numberingId": "488"}}, {"id": "body-24085", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "CXR", "runs": [{"text": "CXR"}], "list": {"level": 0, "numberingId": "488"}}, {"id": "body-24086", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "CT scan / MRI of the Abdomen and Pelvis", "runs": [{"text": "CT"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "scan"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "/"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "MRI"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "the"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Abdomen